<!-- SHEET: INTERPRETATION -->
<div class="sheet" id="sh-interp">
  <div class="sh-handle"></div>
  <div class="sh-hdr"><div class="sh-title">&#128269; Interpretation</div><div class="sh-close" onclick="closeSheet('sh-interp')">&times;</div></div>
  <div class="sh-body">
    <div class="ov-sec">
      <div class="ov-h">How to read this map</div>
      <p class="ov-p">Each coloured polygon represents one of Hai Phong's administrative districts. Colour signals flood risk. A small red district is more dangerous than a large yellow one.</p>
      <div class="ov-card"><strong>Polygon colour</strong><span>Reflects the combined score (1&ndash;5) from elevation, water proximity, typhoon history, and infrastructure ratings.</span></div>
      <div class="ov-card"><strong>Tap any district</strong><span>Opens a popup with the verified incident that justifies the score &mdash; a real recorded event with source citation.</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="ov-sec">
      <div class="ov-h">Suitable vs. unsuitable zones</div>
      <span class="ov-tag tag-g">&#10003; Suitable</span>
      <p class="ov-p">Kien An, Thuy Nguyen, An Duong &mdash; elevated terrain, manageable seasonal exposure. Standard development with normal insurance.</p>
      <span class="ov-tag tag-o">&#9888; Conditional</span>
      <p class="ov-p">Hong Bang, Le Chan, Ngo Quyen &mdash; urban core with drainage constraints. Viable only with flood-resilient design.</p>
      <span class="ov-tag tag-r">&#10005; Unsuitable</span>
      <p class="ov-p">Do Son, Hai An, Tien Lang, Duong Kinh &mdash; direct coastal surge paths or delta flooding. High-value development not recommended without major mitigation.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="ov-sec">
      <div class="ov-h">Mitigation priorities</div>
      <div class="ov-card"><strong>Dike reinforcement</strong><span>Do Son, Duong Kinh &mdash; MARD 2021: dike systems sub-standard for Category 3+ typhoon loading.</span></div>
      <div class="ov-card"><strong>Drainage upgrade</strong><span>Hong Bang, Le Chan &mdash; legacy networks overwhelmed at 80mm/hr. Investment required before densification.</span></div>
      <div class="ov-card"><strong>Elevation compensation</strong><span>Tien Lang, Vinh Bao &mdash; new construction should be raised minimum 1.5m above current grade.</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SHEET: IMPLICATIONS -->
<div class="sheet" id="sh-impl">
  <div class="sh-handle"></div>
  <div class="sh-hdr"><div class="sh-title">&#128204; Risk Implications</div><div class="sh-close" onclick="closeSheet('sh-impl')">&times;</div></div>
  <div class="sh-body">
    <div class="ov-sec">
      <div class="ov-h">For lenders &amp; MICs</div>
      <div class="ov-card"><strong>Collateral impairment</strong><span>Properties in Very High zones carry documented flood damage histories. Values may not reflect latent flood liability.</span></div>
      <div class="ov-card"><strong>LTV adjustment</strong><span>Standard 70&ndash;80% LTV is inappropriate for Very High districts without flood insurance confirmation. Recommend 55&ndash;65% cap.</span></div>
      <div class="ov-card"><strong>Portfolio concentration</strong><span>30%+ of portfolio in Very High districts creates correlated risk &mdash; a single typhoon can trigger simultaneous defaults.</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="ov-sec">
      <div class="ov-h">For insurers</div>
      <div class="ov-card"><strong>Single-peril gap</strong><span>Standard policies cover river overflow OR storm surge &mdash; not both simultaneously. Five districts face compound events. This gap is not priced.</span></div>
      <div class="ov-card"><strong>Island isolation premium</strong><span>Cat Hai assets require a post-event accessibility surcharge. Adjusters cannot reach these districts for 2&ndash;5 days post-typhoon.</span></div>
      <div class="ov-card"><strong>Industrial liability</strong><span>Thuy Nguyen floods spread contaminants into adjacent agricultural land, creating third-party liability claims beyond the insured boundary.</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="ov-sec">
      <div class="ov-h">For developers</div>
      <div class="ov-card"><strong>Design standards</strong><span>Construction in High or Very High zones must reference Vietnam MARD flood resilience guidelines. Raised foundations non-negotiable in red zones.</span></div>
      <div class="ov-card"><strong>Permit risk</strong><span>Vietnam's National Climate Change Strategy (2021) signals tightening controls in coastal flood zones over 20-year horizons.</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SHEET: RECOMMENDATIONS -->
<div class="sheet" id="sh-rec">
  <div class="sh-handle"></div>
  <div class="sh-hdr"><div class="sh-title">&#128101; Recommendations</div><div class="sh-close" onclick="closeSheet('sh-rec')">&times;</div></div>
  <div class="sh-body">
    <div class="ov-sec">
      <div class="ov-h">For insurers</div>
      <div class="cl-card"><div class="cl-ct">Immediate actions</div><p>&bull; Require compound flood cover (surge + river overflow) for all new commercial policies in Very High districts<br>&bull; Add Cat Hai island isolation rider to all policies there<br>&bull; Commission elevation surveys for policies above $500K USD in red zones</p></div>
      <div class="cl-card"><div class="cl-ct">Portfolio strategy</div><p>Apply a 25&ndash;35% premium loading in Very High districts vs. Medium districts. Tien Lang and Duong Kinh are currently underpriced relative to documented loss history.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="ov-sec">
      <div class="ov-h">For developers</div>
      <div class="cl-card"><div class="cl-ct">Site selection priority</div><p>1. Kien An &amp; Thuy Nguyen &mdash; lowest flood exposure<br>2. An Duong &mdash; residential viable with standard drainage<br>3. Ngo Quyen &mdash; viable with drainage investment<br>4. Avoid Very High districts without major dike works</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="ov-sec">
      <div class="ov-h">For lenders / MICs</div>
      <div class="cl-card"><div class="cl-ct">Underwriting policy</div><p>&bull; Very High: flood insurance confirmation + independent appraisal required<br>&bull; High: flood risk disclosure; cap LTV at 65%<br>&bull; Medium: standard underwriting with annual review<br>&bull; Flag portfolio concentration above 20% in any single risk tier</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="ov-sec">
      <div class="ov-h">For local government</div>
      <div class="cl-card"><div class="cl-ct">Infrastructure priority</div><p>1. Do Son &amp; Duong Kinh dike upgrade &mdash; highest return on risk reduction<br>2. Hong Bang &amp; Le Chan drainage modernisation<br>3. Cat Hai pre-positioned emergency supply depot<br>4. Tien Lang managed retreat planning</p></div>
    </div>
    <p class="disc">Informational guidance only. Does not constitute legal, engineering, or financial advice. Contact intelliwhere@gmail.com for a site-specific assessment.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SHEET: CITY CONTEXT -->
<div class="sheet" id="sh-city">
  <div class="sh-handle"></div>
  <div class="sh-hdr"><div class="sh-title">&#127961;&#65039; City Context</div><div class="sh-close" onclick="closeSheet('sh-city')">&times;</div></div>
  <div class="sh-body">
    <div class="ov-sec">
      <div class="ov-h">Why Hai Phong floods</div>
      <p class="ov-p">Hai Phong sits at the mouth of the Red River Delta where Vietnam's largest river system meets the Gulf of Tonkin. Most urban areas sit just 1&ndash;4 metres above sea level. Three flood mechanisms converge simultaneously:</p>
      <div class="stat-c"><strong>Typhoons</strong> &mdash; The Gulf of Tonkin is one of Asia's most active typhoon corridors. Storms funnel directly toward Hai Phong's coast.</div>
      <div class="stat-c"><strong>River overflow</strong> &mdash; The Thai Binh and Van Uc rivers drain the entire northern highlands and back up into low-lying districts after monsoon rain.</div>
      <div class="stat-c"><strong>Storm surge</strong> &mdash; Typhoon winds push seawater inland. A 1m surge typical in a Category 2 storm inundates most of the urban core.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="ov-sec">
      <div class="ov-h">Historical impact</div>
      <div class="stat-c"><strong>14 significant typhoons</strong> within 100km of Hai Phong between 1990 and 2024 (NOAA IBTrACS)</div>
      <div class="stat-c"><strong>45,000 evacuated</strong> during Typhoon Mirinae (2009) &mdash; Do Son peninsula submerged under 3m surge</div>
      <div class="stat-c"><strong>12,000 homes flooded</strong> in Hai An during Typhoon Son Tinh (2018) &mdash; port gates overtopped</div>
      <div class="stat-c"><strong>$4M+ USD crop loss</strong> in An Lao when Thai Binh River dikes failed 2017 (FAO Vietnam)</div>
    </div>
    <div class="ov-sec">
      <div class="ov-h">Climate trajectory</div>
      <div class="stat-c">Sea level rise of <strong>20&ndash;30cm by 2050</strong> projected for the Red River Delta coast (UNDP Vietnam 2019)</div>
      <div class="stat-c">Typhoon intensification: storms reaching higher wind speeds faster due to warmer sea temperatures</div>
      <div class="stat-c">Monsoon rainfall increasingly concentrated into shorter, more intense events &mdash; overwhelming legacy drainage</div>
    </div>
    <div class="ov-sec">
      <div class="ov-h">Key incident timeline</div>
      <p class="ov-p"><span class="yb">2005</span><span style="color:#c8d6e8">Thai Binh River dike breach &mdash; An Lao flooded</span></p>
      <p class="ov-p"><span class="yb">2009</span><span style="color:#c8d6e8">Typhoon Mirinae &mdash; 45,000 evacuated, 3m surge Do Son</span></p>
      <p class="ov-p"><span class="yb">2017</span><span style="color:#c8d6e8">River flood &mdash; Vinh Bao 8,000 displaced, $4M crop loss</span></p>
      <p class="ov-p"><span class="yb">2018</span><span style="color:#c8d6e8">Typhoon Son Tinh &mdash; Hai An port overtopped, 12,000 homes</span></p>
      <p class="ov-p"><span class="yb">2022</span><span style="color:#c8d6e8">200mm rainfall &mdash; Hong Bang 1,500 homes flooded</span></p>
      <p class="ov-p"><span class="yb">2024</span><span style="color:#c8d6e8">Typhoon Bebinca &mdash; Cat Hai evacuated, 72hr ferry cutoff</span></p>
    </div>
    <div class="ov-sec">
      <div class="ov-h">Data sources</div>
      <p class="ov-p" style="color:#2a3d55;font-size:11px;line-height:1.9">OpenStreetMap &middot; NASA SRTM 30m &middot; NOAA IBTrACS &middot; ReliefWeb / UN OCHA &middot; FAO Vietnam &middot; Vietnam MARD &middot; VnExpress &middot; Tuoi Tre &middot; UNDP Vietnam 2019</p>
    </div>
    <p class="disc">Prepared by Ryan Nguyen, B.Eng Geomatics Engineering (EIT) &mdash; intelliwhere@gmail.com</p>
  </div>
</div>

